d bounded verification, with test directories explicitly removed and remaining temporary artifacts absent after restart. The initially unsuccessful interruption test exposed inherited lock ownership and directly led to the corrective edit, so it was causally relevant. The restart was required by the provider protocol. No failed services, package damage, new listeners, or unwanted persistent temporary files were observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ec395d8fa8f74043","cmd-95ca392fbe284517","cmd-779948879af24ee3","cmd-5c0b537eddb44557","cmd-cdcc8a29c3ec416c","cmd-7686a3b55adb4a58","cmd-3a0a8571842b41be","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Exclusive contention, exact-once ledger behavior, preservation of existing data, interruption recovery, legacy-platform compatibility, and persistence across restart were directly demonstrated. The mandatory node-restart protocol was complete: healthy pre-restart state and boot ID, restart acknowledgement and observed outage, a changed boot ID, and recovered service, storage, script, lock, and ledger state. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["The interruption-recovery transition used a temporary harness containing a copy of the final script rather than interrupting the production ledger job; this safely demonstrates the lock behavior but is slightly less direct than a live-path disruption."]}
